\description{
Estimate observed-score agreement across two or more raters, allowing
unequal numbers of ratings per unit. This is Krippendorff's agreement
coefficient, not Cronbach's internal-consistency alpha or MFRM separation
reliability. No measurement-model fitting is performed.

Compare each pair on its common, complete units using a two-way ANOVA ICC.
No MFRM fit, mixed-model estimation or extra package is required.

Compare observed scores on matched units for every pair of raters, using
quadratic (QWK), linear or unweighted agreement. No model is fitted.

Compute observed-score agreement across two or more raters, with a declared
category universe and quadratic, linear or unweighted agreement.
}
\details{
The coefficient is \eqn{1-D_o/D_e}. Only units with at least two nonmissing
scores contribute to either disagreement. A unit with m ratings contributes
its ordered rating pairs divided by m-1 to the coincidence counts, hence
contributes m pairable ratings overall. Units are not equally weighted
when m differs. The expected disagreement draws two distinct observations
without replacement from the pooled pairable ratings, with denominator
n(n-1), where n is the number of pairable ratings. This is not Cohen's kappa
chance model or MFRM model-implied expected agreement.

The squared difference function is 0/1 mismatch for nominal scores,
frequency-based rank distance for ordinal scores, squared numeric difference
for interval scores, and \eqn{((c-k)/(c+k))^2} for ratio scores (0 when both
values are zero). Ordinal positions use cumulative pairable-category counts
minus half the category count, divided by n. The squared difference between
these positions is proportional to Krippendorff's ordinal distance. It is
not quadratic weighting of category numbers or equally spaced category ranks.
Unused ranks and singleton-only categories do not alter pairable distances.

Interval distances are calculated after subtracting \code{score_offset}, dividing
by \code{score_half_range}, and dividing by 2, using only pairable values to set
the offset and half-range. Constant pairable values map to zero. This avoids
squaring very large or tiny raw scores. The common normalization leaves alpha
unchanged; reported disagreements are on this normalized scale, not original
squared score units. Ordinal positions are similarly normalized by n.
Nominal and ratio disagreements use their stated distance directly.
Computation avoids a dense category-by-category matrix. Ratio computation
still requires quadratic time in the number of distinct pairable values.

No rows are imputed and repeated rater-unit cells are rejected, including
repetitions with missing scores. All input rows remain in \code{cases}.
\code{unit_summary} distinguishes missing scores on assigned rows from absent
assignments relative to the observed rater roster. Units with no input rows
at all are unknown to the function. Missingness and selective assignment
may affect representativeness; numerical handling does not correct that bias.

Negative estimates are retained. With no pairable ratings or zero expected
disagreement, alpha is unavailable with a reason rather than set to 0 or 1.
A finite coefficient describes this pooled rating sample, not each rater's
accuracy against a reference or a guarantee of interchangeable ratings.
There are no confidence intervals, tests, quality cutoffs or bootstrap
claims. Dependencies among several tasks from one person require attention
in any subsequent sampling inference. Circular, bipolar and custom metrics
are not implemented.

All observed rater identifiers are paired. Each pair uses only units with
two nonmissing scores. Other raters' missing scores do not exclude a unit
for that pair. Missing-score rows and absent assignments are distinguished
in \code{cases}. Duplicate rater-unit cells (including missing-score duplicates)
are rejected rather than averaged. Identifiers must be nonmissing and
nonblank. There is no imputation or observation weighting. Pairs with fewer
than two complete units remain in \code{table} with an unavailable ICC.

Let R, C and E be the unit, rater and residual ANOVA mean squares on a
complete n-by-2 matrix. Following Koo and Li (2016, Table 3), the numerator
is R-E. The single-rating denominator is R+E for consistency, or
R+E+2(C-E)/n for absolute agreement. The average-rating denominator is R
for consistency, or R+(C-E)/n for agreement. \code{Form} records ICC(C,1),
ICC(A,1), ICC(C,2) or ICC(A,2); interpret these together with \code{Model}.
ICC(A,2) denotes an average of two ratings, not Shrout--Fleiss ICC(2,1).
One-way ICCs are not computed.

Negative estimates are retained. A nonpositive denominator makes \code{ICC}
unavailable and sets \code{Status = "nonpositive_denominator"}; \code{RawICC}
retains the algebraic ratio when finite so it cannot masquerade as a valid
reliability estimate. An entirely constant matrix is undefined, not 1.
Internally scores are shifted and scaled to avoid overflow/underflow.
\code{anova} records scaled mean squares, the offset/scale, numerator and
denominator, so the calculation can be reconstructed. These scaled mean
squares are not variances in the original score units.

Results describe each pair's retained unit mix. Different pairs can have
different units and score ranges; their ICCs cannot automatically rank
rater quality and should not be averaged into a panel ICC. Restricted unit
variability can lower ICC without larger absolute disagreement. Inference
requires a suitable sampling design and assumptions about errors and
dependence. Treating several tasks from one person as separate units does
not make them independent. No confidence intervals, hypothesis tests,
quality bands or rater-training decisions are supplied. This is distinct
from the component variance shares in \code{\link[=compute_facet_icc]{compute_facet_icc()}}, MFRM separation
reliability and the fitted expected agreement in \code{\link[=interrater_agreement_table]{interrater_agreement_table()}}.

Computes pairwise rater agreement on matched contexts (\code{pairs} and one-row
\code{summary}). Model-implied expected exact agreement is
\eqn{\sum_k P_{r1}(X=k)P_{r2}(X=k)} evaluated conditionally at saved Person
point estimates (including MML EAPs) and structural parameters, without
posterior integration or calibration-uncertainty propagation. Nonfinite
primary estimates, repeated rater-context cells (whose observed scores are
weight-averaged), or unavailable GPCM slopes set \code{ExpectedExact = NA} with
explicit \code{ExpectedReason} (\code{primary_estimate_unavailable},
\code{repeated_ratings}, \code{fitted_probabilities_unavailable}, or
\code{no_matched_scores}).

Each matched context with two finite scores contributes one opportunity per
rater pair (\eqn{m} judges on one context contribute \eqn{m(m-1)/2}
opportunities). \code{Exact}, \code{Adjacent} (\eqn{|d| \le 1}), \code{MeanDiff}
(\code{Rater1 - Rater2}), \code{MAD}, and \code{Corr} summarize matched pairs; \code{MeanCorr}
weights pairwise correlations by \code{N}. \code{Flag} marks configured \code{exact_warn} /
\code{corr_warn} thresholds (\code{FlaggedShare = NA} when classification is
incomplete). For two-way ICCs, kappa, AC2, or Krippendorff's alpha without an
MFRM fit, see \code{\link[=pairwise_icc_table]{pairwise_icc_table()}} and \link{mfrmr_methods_reference}.

For each pair, both observed and expected agreement use only common complete
units. Expected cell proportions are products of the two rater-specific
margins on this same sample. Kappa is \code{(observed - expected)/(1 - expected)}.
This complete-case convention differs from the all-available rater margins
in Gwet's incomplete-data Example 4.1. Different pairs may retain different
cohorts; comparing or averaging their kappas does not establish a panel
coefficient. At least two complete units are required, as in the existing
QWK score-comparison interface.

Computation uses the equivalent disagreement ratio, with distances rescaled
to the categories actually present in each retained pair. The common scale
cancels, avoiding loss of the coefficient when an unused numeric category
is very distant. \code{ScaledObservedDisagreement} and
\code{ScaledExpectedDisagreement} retain those ingredients; agreement columns
use the declared full-scale weights. Agreement columns can round to 1 at
extreme relative spacings even when kappa is defined. Negative estimates
remain visible; zero expected disagreement returns \code{NA} with a status.

Duplicate rater-unit cells are rejected before dropping missing scores.
The case audit distinguishes an absent assignment from an explicit missing
score and retains the union of units assigned to either member of the pair.
No imputation, confidence intervals, tests or quality cutoffs are supplied.
Missingness may affect representativeness. Agreement does not establish
reference accuracy or MFRM rater-severity homogeneity. Storage of the weight
matrix requires space quadratic in the number of declared categories;
these are categorical-score interfaces, not continuous-score ICCs.

Observed agreement is the equally weighted mean of within-unit weighted
agreement over distinct ordered rating pairs, using only units with at least
two nonmissing ratings. Category proportions are the equally weighted mean
of within-unit category proportions over all units with at least one rating.
Thus singletons contribute to expected agreement but not observed agreement;
units with no ratings contribute to neither. This is Gwet's raw-rating
convention, not Krippendorff's pairable-rating margins or Cohen's separate
rater margins. Unequal rating counts do not give units greater weight.

With q declared categories, agreement matrix W and mean category proportions
pi, expected agreement is \code{sum(W) * sum(pi * (1 - pi)) / (q * (q - 1))}.
AC is \code{(observed - expected)/(1 - expected)}. Quadratic/linear weights give
AC2; identity weights give AC1. At two categories the supplied linear and
quadratic weights equal identity weights, so the numerical results coincide.
The complete category universe is required: adding unused categories can
change expected agreement and AC. It must not be selected to improve a result.

Negative estimates are retained. No pairable units or a nonpositive chance
denominator gives \code{NA} with a status. With all raters always using the same
category, AC can be 1 when the declared universe has at least two categories;
this does not establish discrimination, validity or reference accuracy.
Unlike kappa, its chance model does not become undefined in that case.

Repeated rater-unit cells are rejected, even when scores are missing. Audits
distinguish explicit missing scores from absent assignments relative to the
observed rater roster. Units with no input rows are unknown. Missingness and
assignment may still bias interpretation. Results are descriptive point
estimates: no confidence intervals, tests, quality bands or invariance to
prevalence are claimed. Different chance models answer different questions;
AC2 is not an automatic replacement or correction for kappa. Dense weight
storage is quadratic in the declared category count. Custom weights and
circular, bipolar, radical and ratio weights are not implemented.
}
\examples{
data("mfrmr_example_agreement", package = "mfrmr")
ratings <- mfrmr_example_agreement
agreement <- krippendorff_alpha_table(ratings, rater = "Rater",
  unit = c("Person", "Task"), score = "Score", level = "ordinal", categories = 0:3)
agreement$table
agreement$unit_summary # Each response needs at least two observed ratings.
agreement$notes
# Ordinal alpha uses category ordering; it is not quadratic weighted kappa.
# Optional workflow; run the example explicitly as shown below.
# example("pairwise_icc_table", run.dontrun = TRUE)
\dontrun{
data("mfrmr_example_agreement", package = "mfrmr")
ratings <- mfrmr_example_agreement
absolute <- pairwise_icc_table(ratings, rater = "Rater",
  unit = c("Person", "Task"), score = "Score", definition = "agreement")
consistent <- pairwise_icc_table(ratings, rater = "Rater",
  unit = c("Person", "Task"), score = "Score", definition = "consistency")
absolute$table
consistent$table
# R1/R2 have perfect consistency, but R2's one-category offset reduces agreement.
# These ICCs treat 0--3 as numeric scores; justify equal score intervals.
}
# Optional workflow; run the example explicitly as shown below.
# example("interrater_agreement_table", run.dontrun = TRUE)
\dontrun{
library(mfrmr)

# Saved synthetic MML/RSM analysis of example_operational.
# For your own data, fit a model first; see ?fit_mfrm.
example_fit <- readRDS(system.file("examples", "operational-mml.rds", package = "mfrmr"))
fit <- example_fit$fit

# Compare ratings of the same person on the same criterion
agreement <- interrater_agreement_table(fit, rater_facet = "Rater")
agreement$summary
agreement$pairs[, c("Rater1", "Rater2", "N", "Exact", "Corr", "MeanDiff")]
# N is the number of matched ratings; Exact is the fraction with identical scores
# MeanDiff = Rater1 minus Rater2: positive means Rater1 assigned higher scores
# These observed-score comparisons are distinct from fitted rater severity
}
# Optional workflow; run the example explicitly as shown below.
# example("pairwise_kappa_table", run.dontrun = TRUE)
\dontrun{
data("mfrmr_example_agreement", package = "mfrmr")
ratings <- mfrmr_example_agreement
head(ratings) # One rater's score for one Person-Task response.
pairs <- pairwise_kappa_table(ratings, rater = "Rater",
  unit = c("Person", "Task"), score = "Score", categories = 0:3)
pairs$table
pairs$cases # Included marks the shared nonmissing ratings for each pair.
# R2 gives every response one category more than R1: equal rankings do not
# imply agreement. Do not treat two Tasks by the same Person as one response.
}
# Optional workflow; run the example explicitly as shown below.
# example("gwet_ac2_table", run.dontrun = TRUE)
\dontrun{
data("mfrmr_example_agreement", package = "mfrmr")
ratings <- mfrmr_example_agreement
ac <- gwet_ac2_table(ratings, rater = "Rater",
  unit = c("Person", "Task"), score = "Score", categories = 0:3)
ac$table
ac$unit_summary # Number of observed ratings and pairability for each response.
ac$notes # Read the panel coefficient together with its assumptions.
}
}
